# Cadmium sulfide nanoparticles compositing with chitosan and metal-organic framework: Enhanced photostability and increased carbon dioxide reduction

Ying Zhang1,2 · Hao Liang1,2
Abstract
The utilization of photocatalytic enzyme-coupled systems is considered a promising solution to the solar energy conversion challenge, but it is hampered by its low cofactor regeneration efficiency. Herein, a biomimetic catalytic system was designed inspired by photosynthesis for cofactor regeneration and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. The Rh complexes were anchored in the core of metal-organic framework UiO67 and chitosan (CS) on the $\mathrm{UiO}67@$ Rh surface skillfully helped the in situ growth of CdS. In this core-shell nanoreactor, $\mathrm{UiO}67@$ Rh accepted the electrons produced by CdS for cofactor regeneration. CS acted as a bifunctional bio-scaffold to attach CdS and prevent CdS photocorrosion. Besides, CS spatially separated Rh complexes and CdS, preventing deactivation of Rh complexes by CdS and leakage of Rh complexes. Surprisingly, CS improved the photocatalytic activity and photostability of CdS, while other biomacromolecules did not have this function. The photocatalyst regeneration NADH cooperated with formate dehydrogenase (FDH) for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. The results showed that $0.72\mathrm{mM}$ formic acid was produced in $5\mathrm{h}$ $\mathrm{(TOF{=}0.14\ m m o l/m g/h)}$ , which was triple as much as in the system of $\operatorname{UiO}67\textcircled{a}$ $\operatorname{Rh}@\operatorname{CdS}$ and FDH. This cyclic process could be repeated up to 10 time cycles, where long-term stability of the system was observed and the active state of the enzymes was preserved. This study demonstrated a new and promising organometallic photocatalyst-based artificial cofactor regeneration system, paving the path for further research into the bespoke synthesis of fine chemicals and solar fuels from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ utilizing solar energy.
Keywords  Cofactor regeneration $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\varepsilon}$ CdS photocatalysis $\cdot\cdot$ Enzyme catalysis $\cdot\cdot$ Rhodium complexes $\cdot\cdot$ Photo-biocatalytic systems
## 1  Introduction
Sustainable energy is the current world trend in scientific research and production, which is moving towards high selectivity, operational simplicity, cost-effectiveness, ecofriendliness, and low energy consumption [1, 2]. Biocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -based fuel synthesis is an ideal approach for producing sustainable energy carriers, and it also offsets the rising $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ level in the atmosphere [3–5]. Formic acid is a stable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction intermediate, which could be applied to a fluid energy carrier for fuel cells, a hydrogen storage material, or a raw feedstock for the manufacture of fine chemicals [6–8]. In biocatalysis, enzymatic systems usually require fewer synthetic steps and lower reaction temperatures, and more environmentally friendly solvents, making them the most ideal methods for producing formic acid. Among various enzymes, oxidoreductases are widely employed as one of the most important enzymes to catalyze redox reactions. However, such a procedure is usually accompanied by the engagement of cofactors, such as nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (phosphate) $(\mathrm{NAD}(\mathrm{P})\mathrm{H})$ , to sustain $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction [5]. Given the high cost, stoichiometric usage, and chemical instability of $\mathrm{{NAD}(P)H}$ , it is imperative to develop an alternative cofactor regeneration pathway.
Designing high-performance photocatalysts is the key to improving the regeneration efficiency of cofactors. In a broad range of photocatalysts, CdS, a visible-light-sensitive photocatalyst, is one of the most well-known semiconductor photocatalysts [17, 18]. CdS has excellent carrier transport capabilities, allowing photogenerated electron holes to flow swiftly and effectively, extending photogenerated carriers’ life, and having a high photocatalytic activity [17]. Despite its impressive benefits, the photocorrosion defect of CdS severely restricts its application, and the sulfide is readily oxidized by photogenerated holes, which resulting in the destruction of the lattice structure in CdS [8, 17, 19]. Thus, there is a critical need to come up with a new strategy to enhance CdS photostability. It is worth mentioning that natural macromolecules could effectively curb the escape of $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ in photochemical CdS reactions [20, 21]. However, the strategy to organically combine the synthesis of CdS nanoparticles with the protective effect of natural macromolecules still lacks. As a biomacromolecule, chitosan (CS) may be an ideal carrier for CdS stabilization due to its unique functional structure, superior performance, and diverse functions. Especially, CS contains numerous active hydroxyl (-OH) and amino $\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ groups, beneficial for heavy metal adsorption [22, 23]. This characteristic makes it possible to achieve the in situ CdS growth on CS. On the other hand, from the perspective of structural bionics, CS can simulate the structure of biological membranes to spatially separate CdS and Rh complexes, avoiding the toxic effect of CdS on Rh complexes and the leakage of Rh complexes.
