## 2.2.1  Preparation of UiO67@Rh@CS
In $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of $1\%$ acetic acid solution, $0.15\mathrm{~g~}$ chitosan was scattered and swirled until completely dissolved. Then, $50~\mathrm{mg~UiO67@Rh}$ was added to the solution by swirling at $400\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The product was centrifuged, and then the precipitate was washed three times with $1\%$ acetic acid and deionized water, respectively. The thickness of the chitosan shell was regulated by changing the amount of chitosan.
## 2.2.2  Preparation of UiO67@Rh@CS@CdS
$\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ $\mathrm{50~mg)}$ ) was dispersed in $10\mathrm{mMCdCl}_{2}$ solution and reacted at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $300\mathrm{rpm}$ for $2\mathrm{~h~}$ . The solution was centrifuged and washed with water and then dispersed into $1\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ solution, adsorbed at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $300~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . Finally, the products were centrifuged and washed three times with water to obtain U $\mathrm{iO}67\ @\mathrm{Rh}@$ $\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ . As a control, the $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CdS}$ without chitosan was synthesized by the same method. The concentration of $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ remained unchanged, and the concentration of $S^{2-}$ was changed from 1 to $15~\mathrm{mM}$ to regulate the ratio of CdS concentration.
## 2.2.3  NADH regeneration
The reaction medium included $50~\mathrm{mM}$ HEPES buffer $\mathrm{(pH=7.0)}$ , $0.4{\bf M}$ TEOA, $2\mathrm{mMNAD^{+}}$ , and $\mathrm{JiO67}@\mathrm{Rh}@$ $\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ with a total volume of $2\mathrm{mL}$ . The reaction system was illumined for $30\mathrm{min}$ under $300\mathrm{W}$ Xe lamp $\lambda{>}420\mathrm{nm})$ and then centrifuged. The absorbance value of the supernatant was measured at $340\mathrm{nm}$ using a UV-vis spectrophotometer, and the NADH concentration was calculated from the standard curve (Fig. S10).
## 2.2.4  Photo‑enzyme coupling systems
In $5~\mathrm{mL}$ reaction medium, $2\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{NAD^{+}}$ , $\operatorname{UiO}67\ @\operatorname{Rh}\ @$ $\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{Cd}\mathrm{S}$ , formate dehydrogenase (FDH, $1.5\mathrm{U/mg}$ ), $0.4{\bf M}$ TEOA, 0.1 M ${\mathrm{NaCO}}_{3}$ , and $0.2{\bf M}$ HCl were added in HEPES buffer ( $50\mathrm{mM}$ , $\mathrm{pH}7.0\$ ),with ${\mathrm{NaCO}}_{3}$ as a source of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The system was exposed under a 300 W Xe lamp $\lambda>420~\mathrm{nm},$ for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ , and $1\mathrm{mL}$ liquid was collected across the membrane $(0.25\upmu\mathrm{m})$ at regular intervals. The sample was analyzed by HPLC (5 $\mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}0.6\ \mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{min}$ . Column: Aminex 87H $300\times7.8~\mathrm{mm}$ to measure formic acid concentration.
## 3  Results and discussion
## 3.1  Design and characterization of rhodium‑based core‑shell photoreactor
The mediator $[\mathrm{Cp}^{\ast}\mathrm{Rh(bpy)Cll}]^{+}$ has been substantiated to be the most efficacious non-enzymatic catalyst for $\mathrm{{NAD}(P)H}$ regeneration [25, 26]. Compared with free $[\mathrm{Cp}^{\ast}\mathrm{Rh(bpy)Cl}]^{+}$ , its immobilized state is beneficial to maintain high activity. UiO67, containing bipyridine ligands, utilizes bpy groups as a bidentate chelator with metal Rh via a metallization process, resulting in catalytically active $[\mathrm{Cp}^{\ast}\mathrm{Rh(bpy)Cl}]^{+}$ [27, 28].
The morphology of the samples was observed with a scanning electron microscope (SEM), and transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) were used to get insight into the microstructure of samples. As shown in Fig. 1a and b, both UiO67 and $\mathrm{UiO}67\textcircled{a}]$ Rh exhibited typical octahedral structures with smooth surfaces, indicating that the anchoring of Rh complexes had no effect on UiO67 morphology. When the CS shell was assembled, visually, the color of $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ changed to light yellow compared with $\mathrm{UiO}67@$ Rh (Fig.  S2). Additionally, $\mathrm{UiO}67\ @\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ retained an octahedral structure with a rougher surface (Fig. 1c). The TEM image showed that the CS shell thickness of $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ was about $7.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 1f). These results indicated the successful formation of the $\mathrm{UiO}67@$ $\operatorname{Rh}@\operatorname{CS}$ . In the $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ (Fig. 1d), small particles could be seen on the surface of $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ , indicating that CdS was successfully grown on the CS surface. In the HRTEM (Fig. 1h), lattice fringe width of $0.358~\mathrm{nm}$ belonged to the (100) plane of hexagonal CdS [31], which matched well with the PXRD results. EDS mapping analysis (Fig. 1i) revealed the homogeneous distribution of CdS on the outside surface of $\mathrm{UiO}67{\textcircled{\omega}}\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ , further confirming the CdS formation.
Then, the crystal structure and phase alteration of the samples were analyzed by PXRD. According to Fig. S3a, the PXRD pattern of $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{F}$ h was consistent with that of UiO67, implying that the anchoring of Rh complexes had little influence on the crystal structure of the UiO67 core. $\mathrm{UiO}67{\textcircled{\omega}}\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ basically retained the characteristic peaks of UiO67, and a broad peak at $2\theta=20^{\circ}$ corresponds to CS, indicating that the CS shell was successfully assembled. In Fig. 2a, the pure CdS showed that distinct diffraction peaks at $25.0^{\circ}$ , $26.8^{\circ}$ , $28.5^{\circ}$ , $37.0^{\circ}$ , $44.0^{\circ}$ , $47.9^{\circ}$ , and $52.1^{\circ}$ were well aligned to the (100), (002), (101), (102), (110), (103), and (112) crystal planes of hexagonal phase CdS (JCPDS#41-1049) [14]. Intriguingly, in the patterns of $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ , there were three diffraction peaks at $2\theta$ values of $26.8^{\circ}$ , $43.8^{\circ}$ , and $51.7^{\circ}$ ; these peaks were ascribed to the (111), (220), and (311) crystal planes of cubic CdS (JCPDS#10-0454) [23]. The emergence of cubic phase CdS in $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ could be attributed to the fact that CS promoted the reaction rate of $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ and $\mathbf{S}^{2-}$ in the precursor, allowing cubic phase CdS to grow [32]. During the synthesis of CdS, the $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ ions of the precursor matched with S atoms to form a Cd-S coordination bond because the S atoms had a coordinated lone pair of electrons. However, since the N atom was more electronegative than the S atom, it competed with the S atom for $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ coordination to generate a Cd-N bond [23]. The formation of the Cd-N coordination bond probably resulted in the orientation-attachment of $\mathrm{[CdS_{4}-N]}$ coordinated cluster on CS. This changed the Cd-S bond length and caused the distortion of the $\mathrm{CdS}_{4}$ tetrahedral unit, further inducing the crystal phase transition of CdS from hexagonal wurtzite to cubic sphalerite [32].
The chemical bond changes in $\mathrm{UiO}67\ @\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ and $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ were further confirmed by FTIR spectroscopy. UiO $)67@\mathbf{Rh}@\mathbf{CS}$ showed a new absorption band at $484~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ induced by $Z\mathrm{r-O}$ stretching vibrations compared to $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}$ , indicating the interaction between CS and $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{RF}$ . The absorption band obtained at $1159\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ for pure CS was associated with C-O stretching. The characteristic band near $1024~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ was attributed to the combined effect of stretching vibrations of C-O of primary alcohols and C-N of primary amines, and the band at $894~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ was attributed to the stretching vibrations of the glycoside C-O-C (Fig. S3b). As shown in Fig. 2b, the band at $1592\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was formed by N-H bending of amide II [31], and the transition from a single peak $(1592\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ to a double peak ( $1592\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1637\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) could be related to the emergence of a Cd-N bond. The peak caused by -OH stretching and N-H stretching for $\mathrm{UiO67}@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ at $3423~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was slightly shifted to $3431~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS@CdS.}$ This was caused by the interaction of the $\left.-\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right.$ in the CS and $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ . There was the probability of a -OH-S weak hydrogen bond forming [33], verifying the connection between CS and CdS. XPS was further used to investigate the chemical environment of $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ and $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@$ CdS. The signal of $\mathrm{C-N=C}$ in pyridine rings could be seen in high resolution XPS C 1s and $\mathbf{N}$ 1s spectra (Fig. S4a, b). Especially, significant changes in the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C-N=C}$ and N-Rh signals were most likely caused by the CS-shell formation. In Fig. 2c, the two binding energies at $309.2\mathrm{~eV}$ and $313.8\mathrm{eV}$ in $\mathrm{UiO}67@$ Rh were assigned to Rh $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and Rh
$3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ , respectively [34], while the positions of these two peaks in $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ were shifted positively. Meanwhile, the signal of $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ was weaker than that of $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}$ , which was probably caused by the growth of the CS shell. In Fig. 2d, the Cd 3d high-resolution XPS spectra of $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CdS}$ and $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ were presented. The relationship between CdS and CS was studied with $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CdS}$ as the control. The $\mathrm{UiO}67@$ $\operatorname{Rh}@\operatorname{CdS}$ spectra had two peaks at $402.5\mathrm{eV}$ and $409.2\mathrm{eV}$ , which were attributed to Cd $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and Cd $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ , respectively [14]. Cd $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ $409.5\mathrm{eV})$ and Cd $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ ( $402.8\mathrm{eV})$ binding energies were found to be higher in $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ . Because oxygen and nitrogen are more electronegative than sulfur, CdS growing on CS to form Cd-N and Cd-O bonds reduces the electron cloud density of Cd atoms in $\mathrm{UiO}67@$ $\operatorname{Rh}@\operatorname{CS}@\operatorname{CdS}$ , raising the $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ BE value [35]. A similar BE shift was seen in high-resolution S 2p XPS spectra (Fig. S5a). All of these results supported the effective in situ growth of CdS on UiO67 $@$ Rh $\ @\mathrm{CS}$ .
3.2  The role of CS in stabilizing CdS and optical properties of photoreactor
To reveal the role of CS on the stability of CdS, the transient photocurrent response (TPR) of the samples was measured under visible light $\langle\lambda>420\mathrm{nm}\rangle$ (Fig. 3a). The UiO67 $@$ Rh $@$
CS@CdS photocurrent density remained at a high level after 6 cycles, while the CdS photocurrent density continued to decrease and was lower than that of $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ . It was possible that the optical characteristics of CdS have been reduced by photocorrosion. In $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ , since CS chains contained numerous -OH and - $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ groups, the presence of lone pair electrons in -OH and - $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\bar{\rho}}}_{-\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ might create an environment rich in electrons that made it easier for holes $(\mathrm{h^{+}})$ to be separated from the CdS valence band, delaying photocorrosion of CdS [23]. The charge transfer and recombination of the samples were studied by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS). As shown in Fig. 3b, the radius of $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ was smaller than that of CdS, indicating that $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ has lower resistance. Thus, CS greatly improved the transfer and separation of electron holes. In $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ , CS could guide photogenerated $\mathrm{h^{+}}$ to transfer to CS instead of CdS itself, thereby avoiding the photocorrosion of pure CdS. In addition, with an in situ growth strategy, the $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ -OH complex precursors were initially synthesized by adsorbing $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ ions on CS, and then CdS was generated on the $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}{\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ and $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ -OH complex precursors. This strategy made $\left.-\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right.$ and -OH in CS to be precisely distributed around CdS, which was advantageous for the lone pair of electrons of $\left.-\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right.$ and -OH to attract $\mathrm{h^{+}}$ in the valence band of CdS, thus realizing the spatial separation of photoelectrons and $\mathrm{h^{+}}$ [21, 36]. To further validate the role of functional groups on CS, other biomacromolecules without - ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were selected, such as starch (containing only -OH, named as UiO67@Rh $@$ starch ${\mathcal{@}}\mathrm{Cd}\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ and sodium alginate (containing -OH and -COOH, named as $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@$ $\mathbf{SA@CdS}_{\mathrm{,}}^{\mathrm{}}$ . Intuitively, the color of $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{SA@CdS}$ was slightly deeper before and after synthesis, whereas the color of UiO67@Rh $@$ starch ${\widehat{\ @}}\mathrm{Cd}\mathrm{S}$ did not change. We speculated that CdS did not grow on starch. The optical properties of UiO67@Rh $@$ starch@CdS and $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{SA}@$ CdS were subsequently examined to reveal the role of starch and SA in CdS stability by TPR and EIS. As illustrated in Fig. S6, although the photocurrent density of $\operatorname{UiO}67\ @\operatorname{Rh}\ @$ $\operatorname{SA}@\operatorname{CdS}$ remained constant after multiple cycles, it was significantly lower than that of $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ , and UiO67@Rh $@$ starch $@\mathrm{Cd}\mathrm{S}$ had essentially no photocurrent density value. Furthermore, $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{SA@CdS}$ and U $\mathrm{IiO67@Rh@starch@Cd{\Omega}}$ S also had barely detectable resistance (Fig. S6b). We speculated that $\left.\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\right.$ and -COOH might play a role in the growth and stability of CdS. In $\mathrm{UiO}67@$ $\operatorname{Rh}@\operatorname{CS}@\operatorname{CdS}$ , the - ${\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ promoted the growth of CdS and played a key role in improving photostability, whereas the -OH served a supportive role. The result of FTIR analysis can also explain this phenomenon.
Photoluminescence (PL) spectroscopy characterized the separation effectiveness of photogenerated charges of photocatalysts. Whereafter, to investigate the interfacial charge transfer between CdS and CS, the PL spectroscopy of $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CdS}$ and $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ was measured at an excitation wavelength of $370~\mathrm{nm}$ . As shown in Fig. S7a, $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CdS}$ had a strong emission peak at $532\mathrm{nm}$ , which was the result of its electron-hole recombination. On the contrary, the PL intensity of $\operatorname{UiO}67\ @\operatorname{Rh}\ @$ $\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ was significantly lower than that of $\operatorname{UiO}67\ @\operatorname{Rh}\ @$ CdS, which was caused by the rapid division of electron holes after the growth of CdS on the CS surface. This result indicated that the combination of CdS and CS achieved effective charge separation, which was consistent with the EIS results. To gain a deeper understanding of the dynamic charge transfer between CdS and CS, time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) decay spectra were performed on $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CdS}$ and $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ , as shown in Fig. S7b. By a three-exponential fitting method, the average PL lifetime of $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CdS}$ and $\operatorname{UiO}67\ @\operatorname{Rh}\ @$ $\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ was calculated and presented in Table 1. The average PL lifetime increased from 0.812 $\operatorname{\mathrm{\langleUiO67@Rh}@}$ CdS) to 0.980 ns $(\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS}@\mathrm{CdS})$ , both higher than pure CdS. The prolongation of the average PL lifetime of $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS_{\Delta}@C d S_{\Delta}}$ indicated that the separation efficiency of electron holes was substantially enhanced. This was because $\left.-\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right.$ and -OH on CS delayed the recombination of electron holes, effectively extending electron holes’ lifetimes, inhibiting CdS photocorrosion and facilitating photocatalytic enhancement.
The light absorption characteristics of $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CdS}$ and $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ were studied by UV-vis DRS. The introduction of CdS enhanced the light absorption intensity of $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ in the visible region compared with $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ , contributing to a less bandgap and a significant strengthening of visible light uptake and utilization (Fig. S7c). In addition, the bandgaps of $\mathrm{UiO}67@$ $\operatorname{Rh}@\operatorname{CS}$ and $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ were calculated using the Kubelka-Munk function equation: $\alpha h\nu=A(h\nu-E_{\mathrm{g}})^{2}$ (where $h\nu,\alpha,A$ , and $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ were light energy, absorption index, constant value, and bandgap energy of the semiconductor, respectively) [37]. The differential spectrum transformed was shown in Fig. S7d. Though the extension line of the linear region was crossed with the $\mathbf{\deltaX}$ -axis, the bandgap energies of $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ and $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ were estimated to be $3.37\mathrm{eV}$ and $3.26\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively. The less bandgap and more efficient light absorption of $\mathrm{UiO}67@$ $\operatorname{Rh}@\operatorname{CS}@\operatorname{CdS}$ were beneficial to effectively increase the generation of photogenerated charges, thereby promoting the enhancement of its photocatalytic activity [38].
The flat-band potentials $(E_{\mathrm{fb}})$ of $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ , $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ , and CdS in 0.1 M $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ electrolyte were calculated using Mott-Schottky curves. In Fig. 3c, all samples had a positive slope, indicating that they were all n-type semiconductors. The $E_{\mathrm{fb}}$ of $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS@CdS}$ , and CdS were $-0.97{\mathrm{~V}},-0.96{\mathrm{~V}},$ and $-0.99\mathrm{~V~}$ vs a saturated calomel electrode (SCE), respectively. It is commonly identified that for n-type semiconductors, the conduction band potential $(\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{CB}})$ is usually negative 0.1 or $0.2\mathrm{~V~}$ than its $E_{\mathrm{fb}}$ [39]. Thus, the $E_{\mathrm{CB}}$ for $\operatorname{UiO67}@\operatorname{Rh}@\operatorname{CS}$ and CdS was roughly reckoned up to be $-0.77{\mathrm{~V~}}$ and − 0.79 V. Combining the bandgap and Mott-Schottky results, the valence band potentials $(E_{\mathrm{VB}})$ of $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ and CdS were $2.60\mathrm{eV}$ and $1.25\mathrm{eV}$ by the calculated formula: $\centering E_{V B}=E_{C B}+E_{g}$ [37]. Intuitively, the estimated positions of $E_{\mathrm{CB}}$ and $E_{\mathrm{VB}}$ in $\mathrm{\bar{UiO67@Rh@CS}}$ and CdS were shown in Fig. 3d. The band alignment between $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ and CdS indicated that it was a typical type-ii heterojunction structure [37]. This structure enabled the electrons generated by CdS photoexcitation to flow to $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ under visible light irradiation, which spatially separated the photogenerated charges and enhanced the photocatalytic activity.
## 3.3  UiO67@Rh@CS@CdS as photocatalysts for NADH regeneration
In the catalytic system, NADH regeneration efficiency is one of the determinants of substrate oxidation efficiency. Therefore, a series of photocatalytic conditions were first regulated to optimize NADH regeneration efficiency of $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ , and the results was shown in Fig. S8. The following experiments were carried out under optimal conditions.
The ability of $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ to regenerate NADH from $\mathrm{NAD^{+}}$ under visible light $\mathrm{(>420~nm)}$ ) was first examined by UV-visible absorption spectroscopy. NADH regeneration was detected by the change in the characteristic peak of NADH at $340~\mathrm{nm}$ . As shown in Fig. S9a, the characteristic peak at $340~\mathrm{nm}$ for $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@$ CdS progressively increased with time in HEPES buffer. NADH regeneration experiments on $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@$ CdS, $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CdS}$ , mechanical mixtures of pure CdS, and $\mathrm{UiO67}@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}$ were operated under the same conditions and single-photocatalyst Rh complexes (M) as control group (Fig. S9b). The reaction was first tested in the dark;
no NADH was generated from $\mathrm{NAD^{+}}$ in the dark. When M was used as a single photocatalyst, no NADH was generated, which was because the Rh complexes itself did not produce electrons under light and cannot reduce $\mathrm{NAD^{+}}$ . Compared to the other two regeneration systems, $\mathrm{UiO}67@$ $\operatorname{Rh}@\operatorname{CS}@\operatorname{CdS}$ exhibited the best NADH regeneration ability. These results showed that $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS@CdS}$ achieved NADH regeneration, and $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ constituted a photoreactor. Subsequently, a series of control experiments were performed to exclude the effect of other factors on NADH regeneration. As shown in Fig. S9c, other conditions being equal, a certain condition was changed to observe whether NADH was produced. There was no NADH production in the absence of $\mathrm{NAD^{+}}$ , light, CdS, or M, respectively. A small amount of NADH was produced in the absence of TEOA, because the photocorrosion phenomenon reduced the catalytic efficiency of CdS. Here, TEOA acted as a sacrificial agent to stabilize sulfide ions by consuming photogenerated $\mathrm{h^{+}}$ , inhibiting the photocorrosion of CdS and boosting the photocatalytic activity.
## 3.4  Photo‑enzyme cascade production of formic acid from $\mathbf{co}_{2}$
In the photo-biocatalytic system, $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS@CdS}$ was combined with FDH to continuously reduce $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to formic acid, while $\mathrm{NAD^{+}}$ was reduced to NADH. After the biocatalytic reaction, NADH could be regenerated to $\mathrm{NAD^{+}}$ by FDH to continue the catalytic cycle.
Because of the action of photogenerated holes, CdS had NADH oxidase-like activity in the literature [14]. In order to exclude the effect of NADH oxidation by CdS on enzyme catalysis, we first explored whether pure CdS, $\operatorname{JiO67}@\operatorname{Rh}@$ CdS, and $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ would oxidize NADH. The reduction of NADH was detected at $340\mathrm{nm}$ by replacing $\mathrm{NAD^{+}}$ with NADH in the photocatalytic system. As shown in Fig. S11, pure CdS consumed $0.065\mathrm{\mM}$ NADH after reacting $^{\textrm{1h}}$ , while the amount of NADH oxidized by $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ was almost negligible. This result also demonstrated the advantage of CS in avoiding the oxidation of NADH.
We employed FDH enzyme system, which relied on NADH to oxidize selective substrates, for the photobiological cascade reaction. The designed photobiocatalytic system was tested using FDH, which converted $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and NADH to formic acid and $\mathrm{NAD^{+}}$ . Figure  5a showed $\operatorname{UiO67}@\operatorname{Rh}@\operatorname{CS}@\operatorname{CdS}$ cascaded with FDH to reduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to formic acid. According to Fig.  5b, formic acid was identified in the reaction system of $\mathrm{UiO67}@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ combined with FDH, and $0.72{\mathrm{~mM}}$ formic acid was produced in $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ $(\mathrm{TOF}=0.14\ \mathrm{mmol/mg/h})$ , which was triple as much as in the system of $\mathrm{UiO}67@\mathrm{Rh}@\mathrm{CdS}$ and FDH. This result clearly revealed the superiority of $\operatorname{UiO}67\textcircled{a}$ $\operatorname{Rh}@\operatorname{CS}@\operatorname{CdS}$ photocatalyst over $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CdS}$ photocatalyst. Of note, several studies have revealed that NADH was not completely converted to $\mathrm{NAD^{+}}$ , with the formation of additional by-products (such as NAD dimers). The accumulation of the by-products may inhibit the conversion of substrates [40–42]. To verify this phenomenon, the products of the cascade system were analyzed by $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR. As shown in Fig. S12, in addition to the conventional $\mathrm{NAD^{+}}$ peaks, certain additional peaks were observed, denoted by red and orange circles, which were compatible with the typical $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR peaks of ADPribose and nicotinamide. This result was consistent with previous report [42]. This may account for the lower the TOF of formic acid, but it was still higher than other reported levels (Table 2).
To demonstrate the role of CS in stabilizing CdS and preventing CdS toxicity to Rh, we investigated the activity of photocatalysts and biocatalysts by measuring NADH concentrations. As shown in Fig. 5c, FDH was combined with $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ or $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CdS}$ photocatalysts, to generate NADH and $\mathrm{NAD^{+}}$ alternately by controlling the switching of the light. In the first biocatalytic cycle, the system was irradiated with light for $10\mathrm{min}$ . During this period, NADH production was observed due to $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh@CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ catalyzing $\mathrm{NAD^{+}}$ to regenerate
NADH. The light was then switched off, at which point the main effect of enzyme catalysis occurred. During this time, a decrease in the NADH concentration appeared. This was because NADH was consumed for reduction to $\mathrm{NAD^{+}}$ , but $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was also reduced to formic acid. After tenth cycles, $0.03~\mathrm{mM}$ NADH was also generated. This indicated that CdS was still in its photocatalytic activity ability, and Rh complexes had the ability to regenerate NADH. Besides, the enzyme was still active without deactivation. In contrast, in the $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CdS}$ catalytic system, NADH production and consumption were only observed in the first cycle, and no alternating NADH production was observed in subsequent cycles. It was possible that the photocatalytic capacity was reduced due to the photocorrosion of CdS in the absence of CS. The high toxicity of CdS deactivated the Rh complexes. Without the protection of CS, the Rh complexes leaked and inactivated the enzyme. To sum up, the photoenzyme cascade system we constructed could maintain high enzyme activity. This outcome was also in keeping with the cyclic test (Fig. 5c).
In a cascade reaction, maintaining a high level of enzyme activity is also essential for the continuation of the reaction system. Therefore, FDH activity was investigated using a series of controlled experiments. In Fig. S13, the FDH activity was set to $100\%$ after incubation alone in the buffer for a period of time in the dark (column 1). The enzyme activity lost $20\%$ after a certain period of light (column 2), indicating light had a slight effect on enzyme activity. Under light irradiation, the activity of FDH remained basically unchanged after co-incubation of $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ and FDH in the presence of TEOA for a period of time. This meant that TEOA devoured some of the photogenerated $\mathrm{h^{+}}$ generated by CdS under light exposure, while the rest was transported to CS, thus having no effect on enzyme activity (column 3). In contrast, the loss of FDH activity was severe without TEOA due to the inactivation of the enzyme by photogenerated $\mathfrak{h}^{+}$ (column 4). Similarly, FDH was basically inactive after $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CS}@\mathrm{CdS}$ was replaced by $\mathrm{UiO67@Rh}@\mathrm{CdS}$ and mechanical mixtures of pure CdS and M, which was probably caused by the Rh complexes and CdS inactivating FDH (column 5, 6, 7).
